# gitleaks suppressions.
#
# This repository deliberately carries key material that is not secret:
# LUKS test passphrases in tests/manifest.json, the synthetic keys
# generated by scripts/create-luks-*, and fixture UUIDs. Every one of
# them exists so the crypto read paths can be tested, and none of them
# guards anything.
#
# To suppress a finding, add its fingerprint on its own line below with
# a comment saying why it is safe. gitleaks prints the fingerprint in
# its report; the format is:
#
#   <commit>:<path>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# or, for a finding with no commit (a working-tree scan):
#
#   <path>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# Prefer fixing a real leak over suppressing it. If a credential ever
# does land here, rotate it first -- the history scan will keep finding
# it, and suppressing the finding does not un-publish the secret.
#
# The first false positive, and what this file was created for.
#
# Do not quote the offending source line in a comment here. The first
# version of this entry did exactly that, and gitleaks scans this file
# like any other -- the quoted line came straight back as a fresh
# finding in .gitleaksignore itself, on the line the explanation sat
# on. Describe the shape of the match instead.
#
# The finding is in the VHDX parent locator parser, where a bounds
# check compares the declared length of one metadata pair name against
# a compile-time limit. The generic-api-key rule matches an identifier
# whose name contains one of its trigger words, then a comparison
# operator, then a long token; it scores that token for entropy and
# reports anything above 3.5. Here the token is the name of a public
# constant declared a few hundred lines above the check in the same
# file, and it scores 3.97 because it is long and mixes case classes.
# There is no credential in that line and nothing to rotate.
